% The mapped functions. Argument reordering, arity-keyed templates, and the
% wrappers that make a server's answer match SEL's.

### name: func.text.upper
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
UPPER("abc")
--- expect
UPPER('abc')
===
### name: func.text.len-counts-characters
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
LEN("zażółć")
--- expect
CHAR_LENGTH('zażółć')
===
### name: func.text.find-reorders-its-arguments
--- note
SEL is FIND(needle, hay); SQL is INSTR(hay, needle). A map that could not
reorder would need a wrapper function in every host.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
FIND("a", "banana")
--- expect
INSTR('banana', 'a')
===
### name: func.text.find-three-argument-form-is-a-different-function
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
FIND("a", "banana", 3)
--- expect
LOCATE('a', 'banana', 3)
===
### name: func.text.replace-reorders-three
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
REPLACE("a", "b", "banana")
--- expect
REPLACE('banana', 'a', 'b')
===
### name: func.text.substr-two-arguments
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
SUBSTR("banana", 2)
--- expect
SUBSTRING('banana', 2)
===
### name: func.text.substr-three-arguments
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
SUBSTR("banana", 2, 3)
--- expect
SUBSTRING('banana', 2, 3)
===
### name: func.text.trim-strips-sel-s-four-characters
--- note
SEL strips space, tab, CR and LF. TRIM(BOTH x FROM s) takes one string rather
than a character set, so it cannot express that; REGEXP_REPLACE can.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
TRIM("  x  ")
--- expect
REGEXP_REPLACE('  x  ', '^[ \\t\\r\\n]+|[ \\t\\r\\n]+$', '')
===
### name: func.text.pad
--- note
LPAD truncates an argument already wider than the target width and SEL's PADL
returns it unchanged, so the template guards the call rather than making it.
The guard repeats {0} and {1}: a slot emitted twice is bound twice, which is
what Fragment::bindings() walking the output is for.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
PADL("7", 3, "0")
--- expect
IF(CHAR_LENGTH('7') >= 3, '7', LPAD('7', 3, '0'))
===
### name: func.text.pad-does-not-truncate
--- note
Found by the oracle, not by anyone reading the manual: LPAD('7777', 2, '0') is
'77' and PADL("7777", 2, "0") is "7777".
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
PADR("7777", 2, "0")
--- expect
IF(CHAR_LENGTH('7777') >= 2, '7777', RPAD('7777', 2, '0'))
===
### name: func.num.min-of-one-argument
--- note
LEAST and GREATEST are variadic but need two arguments, and SEL's MIN takes one.
`*` is the arity-keyed template's fallback; `1` is the count that needs its own.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
MIN(1)
--- expect
1
===
### name: func.num.min-of-several
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
MIN(3, 1, 2)
--- expect
LEAST(3, 1, 2)
===
### name: func.number.min-is-a-scalar-not-an-aggregate
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
MIN(3, 1, 2)
--- expect
LEAST(3, 1, 2)
===
### name: func.number.min-is-variadic
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
MIN(5, 4, 3, 2, 1)
--- expect
LEAST(5, 4, 3, 2, 1)
===
### name: func.number.round
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
ROUND(2.5, 0)
--- expect
ROUND(2.5, 0)
===
### name: func.number.isnum-is-a-regex
--- note
\A and \z, not ^ and $. ICU's $ matches before a trailing newline, so
ISNUM("12\n") answered 1 on MariaDB and MySQL where SEL says FALSE -- a value
that is not a number saying it is, which is the wrong direction for a
validation rule to be wrong in. The two servers also disagreed with each other
on \r. See the isnum-anchor note in mysql-family.json.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
ISNUM("-12.50")
--- expect
('-12.50' REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z')
===
### name: func.binary.to-hex-is-lower-cased
--- note
MariaDB's HEX returns upper case and spec §7.7 says lower.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
TO_HEX(TO_UTF8("a"))
--- expect
LOWER(HEX(CAST('a' AS BINARY)))
===
### name: func.binary.base64-strips-the-wrapping
--- note
The doubled CAST is TO_UTF8's own template meeting {binaryCast:0}, and it stays.
Eliding it would mean the translator knowing which templates already produce
bytes, which is exactly the kind of cleverness that has cost this layer defects;
a redundant cast costs a few characters and nothing else.
--- note
TO_BASE64 wraps every 76 characters; SEL's does not.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
ENCODE_BASE64(TO_UTF8("a"))
--- expect
REPLACE(REPLACE(TO_BASE64(CAST('a' AS BINARY)), CHAR(10), ''), CHAR(13), '')
===
### name: func.binary.crc32-is-padded-hex
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
CRC32("hello")
--- expect
LPAD(LOWER(HEX(CRC32(CAST('hello' AS BINARY)))), 8, '0')
===
### name: func.regex.rmatch-forces-collation-and-dotall
--- note
MariaDB's REGEXP follows the operand's collation and is case-insensitive by
default; and its . does not match a newline where SEL's does.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
RMATCH('^a.c$', "abc")
--- expect
('abc' COLLATE utf8mb4_bin REGEXP '(?s)^a.c$')
===
### name: func.regex.rfind
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
RFIND('ó', "zażółć")
--- expect
REGEXP_INSTR('zażółć' COLLATE utf8mb4_bin, '(?s)ó')
===
### name: func.refused.code-is-not-a-code-point
--- note
ORD reads the first character's bytes as an integer. ASCII agrees by accident,
which is worse than disagreeing.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
CODE("a")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
===
### name: func.refused.char-is-the-inverse
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
CHAR(233)
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
===
### name: func.refused.rreplace-replacement-syntax-differs
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
RREPLACE('a', "b", "banana")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
===
### name: func.refused.split-yields-a-list
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
SPLIT("a,b", ",")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
### name: func.bin.reads-its-argument-as-bytes
--- note
SEL's BIN functions take bytes, and a TEXT or NUM argument is coerced through
UTF-8. HEX(1) hexes the NUMBER and answers "1"; SEL answers "31", the hex of the
character. {binaryCast:0} is the lexical form of "read this operand as bytes",
the same shape textCast has for byte comparisons.

Found by the fuzz lane. Every case here used TO_UTF8 explicitly, so nothing in
the suite ever handed one of these a number.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
TO_HEX(1)
--- expect
LOWER(HEX(CAST(1 AS BINARY)))
===
### name: func.bin.length-of-a-number
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
BLEN(2.50)
--- expect
LENGTH(CAST(2.50 AS BINARY))
===
### name: func.regex.unportable-pattern-is-refused-not-thrown
--- note
A pattern outside SEL's portable subset raises SelError in the evaluator, but
only when the call is reached. Translation walks every branch, so it arrives
here from branches the evaluator never takes -- and a SelError escaping
Sql::translate would break what tryTranslate() promises. Found by the fuzz lane,
as a fatal error mid-run.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
RMATCH('\p{L}', "x")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED
===
### name: func.text.ltrim-strips-sel-s-four-characters
--- note
TRIM had a case and its two siblings did not, on any dialect, although all
three are mapped everywhere and all three are the same decision: SEL strips
space, tab, CR and LF, so the template has to name those four and not defer to
whatever the server calls whitespace. MariaDB's own LTRIM strips spaces only,
which is why this is a REGEXP_REPLACE and not a call to it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
LTRIM(" a")
--- expect
REGEXP_REPLACE(' a', '^[ \\t\\r\\n]+', '')
===
### name: func.text.rtrim-strips-sel-s-four-characters
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
RTRIM("a ")
--- expect
REGEXP_REPLACE('a ', '[ \\t\\r\\n]+$', '')
===
### name: func.bin.from-hex
--- note
Mapped on the MySQL family and PostgreSQL, refused on sqlite, and pinned
nowhere -- a partial refusal, which is the shape where a dialect drifts without
anything noticing. The other two arms are in 14-sqlite.sqlt and
15-postgresql.sqlt.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
FROM_HEX("4869")
--- expect
UNHEX('4869')
===
